WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.295
<v Human Services Supervisor>Thanks for making time. I wanted to ask about last

00:00:03.295 --> 00:00:06.590
<v Human Services Supervisor>week's review meeting. You knew more about the caller problem

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:09.885
<v Human Services Supervisor>than anyone there, and you didn't say anything until I

00:00:09.885 --> 00:00:12.192
<v Human Services Supervisor>asked you directly. Help me understand that.

00:00:12.542 --> 00:00:15.764
<v Call-line lead>Honestly, I was doing math. Two senior specialists had already

00:00:15.764 --> 00:00:18.986
<v Call-line lead>said the draft was solid. If I disagreed, would it

00:00:18.986 --> 00:00:22.207
<v Call-line lead>change anything, or would I just be the person who

00:00:22.207 --> 00:00:23.174
<v Call-line lead>slowed things down?

00:00:23.524 --> 00:00:26.850
<v Human Services Supervisor>Those are the two questions people weigh before speaking up.

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:30.175
<v Human Services Supervisor>Is it safe, and will it matter? I thought I'd

00:00:30.175 --> 00:00:32.836
<v Human Services Supervisor>covered both by saying the floor was open.

00:00:33.186 --> 00:00:36.136
<v Call-line lead>An open floor after twenty minutes of senior people talking

00:00:36.136 --> 00:00:39.086
<v Call-line lead>doesn't feel open. When you asked me by name what

00:00:39.086 --> 00:00:42.037
<v Call-line lead>callers were saying about the assessment step, that was different.

00:00:42.037 --> 00:00:44.987
<v Call-line lead>It told me you actually wanted my part of the

00:00:44.987 --> 00:00:45.282
<v Call-line lead>picture.

00:00:45.632 --> 00:00:49.615
<v Human Services Supervisor>There's research on this from intensive care units. Nurses and

00:00:49.615 --> 00:00:53.597
<v Human Services Supervisor>therapists felt less safe speaking up than physicians. In units

00:00:53.597 --> 00:00:57.580
<v Human Services Supervisor>where leaders actively invited input, that gap shrank, and the

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:00.368
<v Human Services Supervisor>people with less status gained the most.

00:01:00.718 --> 00:01:04.044
<v Call-line lead>That tracks. But what I watch most is what happens

00:01:04.044 --> 00:01:07.369
<v Call-line lead>after. Last year I raised something on a different project.

00:01:07.369 --> 00:01:10.695
<v Call-line lead>The memo said everyone supported the plan. That memo is

00:01:10.695 --> 00:01:12.358
<v Call-line lead>why I hesitated last week.

00:01:12.708 --> 00:01:16.800
<v Human Services Supervisor>That's the part leaders underestimate. A study that followed shift

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:20.893
<v Human Services Supervisor>managers for ten months found that openness predicted who spoke

00:01:20.893 --> 00:01:24.985
<v Human Services Supervisor>up later. Openness meant listening and acting on ideas. Being

00:01:24.985 --> 00:01:28.668
<v Human Services Supervisor>inspiring didn't predict it once earlier behavior was counted.

00:01:29.018 --> 00:01:31.586
<v Call-line lead>So the memo matters more than the pep talk.

00:01:31.936 --> 00:01:34.910
<v Human Services Supervisor>It does. This time the memo will say what callers

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:37.884
<v Human Services Supervisor>told you and that you raised it, if you want

00:01:37.884 --> 00:01:40.858
<v Human Services Supervisor>the credit. It will say what changed and who answers

00:01:40.858 --> 00:01:43.832
<v Human Services Supervisor>the question about the eligibility notice. I'd like you to

00:01:43.832 --> 00:01:45.616
<v Human Services Supervisor>read it before it goes up.

00:01:45.966 --> 00:01:49.179
<v Call-line lead>I'd like the credit. And yes, I'll read it. One

00:01:49.179 --> 00:01:52.392
<v Call-line lead>more thing. When you introduce me to the county partners,

00:01:52.392 --> 00:01:55.605
<v Call-line lead>please say my surname the way I do. People usually

00:01:55.605 --> 00:01:55.926
<v Call-line lead>guess.

00:01:56.276 --> 00:01:58.760
<v Human Services Supervisor>Thank you for telling me. Say it for me now,

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:01.245
<v Human Services Supervisor>and tell me how you'd like the rest of the

00:02:01.245 --> 00:02:03.729
<v Human Services Supervisor>introduction to go. I'd rather ask you here than get

00:02:03.729 --> 00:02:05.468
<v Human Services Supervisor>it wrong in front of the partners.

00:02:05.818 --> 00:02:08.610
<v Call-line lead>That's the same thing, really. You asked first, and you

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:11.122
<v Call-line lead>asked where it was easy for me to answer.
